//===- Builtins.def - The one list of required procedures and functions -*- C++ -*-===//
//
//   BUILTIN(Id, Spelling, Kind, Dialects, MinArgs, MaxArgs, Result, ArgKind)
//
//     Id        The BuiltinID enumerator.
//     Spelling  The name in the source, lower case.  Pascal is case
//               insensitive, and the symbol table keys on the folded name.
//     Kind      Proc or Func.
//     Dialects  Which dialects require it, as D_* bits from LangOptions.h.
//     MinArgs   Fewest arguments accepted.
//     MaxArgs   Most accepted; -1 for no upper bound, and also for the ones
//               whose shape is checked where they are lowered rather than here.
//     Result    What a Func returns, as an R_* tag; R_None for a Proc.
//     ArgKind   What EVERY argument must be, as an AK_* tag Sema::
//               checkBuiltinArgKinds enforces generically right after the
//               arity check above -- AK_Any (the default for every row below
//               that has not been migrated yet) means "not checked here",
//               the same as before this column existed.  A builtin whose
//               arguments do not all share one required kind (succ/pred's
//               separate step-count rule, write's per-position textfile
//               rules, ...) stays AK_Any and keeps its check hand-written in
//               checkCallExpr/checkCallStmt, same as a builtin nobody has
//               migrated yet -- this column only ever ADDS a check, it does
//               not take one away from a builtin still on the old path.
//
// This was three lists.  Sema::registerBuiltins declared the names, a static
// table in SemaExpr checked their arity, and codegen dispatched on the spelling
// -- so adding one meant editing three places that had no way to notice each
// other.  The Turbo Pascal dialect adds about eighty more names, which is what
// made three lists worth folding into one.
//
// A name is DECLARED IN EVERY DIALECT, and Dialects says which ones may use it.
// Declaring it everywhere is what lets a program compiled as ISO 7185 be told
// that `card` is an Extended Pascal extension rather than merely undefined;
// registerBuiltins already said that was the intent, but only about ten names
// were declared that way and the other nineteen -- cmplx, position, binding and
// the rest of the Extended Pascal block -- came back as "undefined function".
// That split was not deliberate, and it is gone.
//
// ArgKind's vocabulary (issue #306's own proposed list, cross-checked against
// what SemaExpr.cpp/SemaStmt.cpp's hand-written arms actually test today
// before any of it was wired up):
//
//   AK_Any              No generic check (the default; see above).
//   AK_Numeric          Type::isNumeric(): integer-, real- or complex-type.
//                       Used where a complex argument is accepted too, e.g.
//                       sqrt/sin/cos/exp/ln/arctan (EP §6.7.6.2 widens the
//                       six ISO required functions to complex) and abs/sqr
//                       (whose complex case is a separate return type, not a
//                       separate argument check -- isNumeric() already says
//                       yes for complex either way).
//   AK_NumericNonComplex  isNumeric() with TypeKind::Complex refused --
//                       needed because isNumeric() alone admits complex
//                       (Type.h's own comment on why: the binary-operator
//                       gates want it to).  trunc/round/int/frac and cmplx/
//                       polar's two components all want a real- or
//                       integer-type argument with no complex alternative
//                       (ISO 10206 Table 2 gives the widened six their own
//                       footnote (1) and does not extend it to these).
//   AK_Complex          TypeKind::Complex alone: re/im/arg (ISO 10206 Table
//                       2 footnote (4), narrower than footnote (1) above).
//   AK_Ordinal          Type::isOrdinal(): integer-, boolean-, char-, or
//                       enumeration-type (or a subrange of one).  ord/chr/
//                       odd (ISO §6.6.6.4 transfer functions and §6.6.6.5)
//                       all read or produce an ordinal position and share
//                       this one hand-written predicate exactly as it stood
//                       before migration -- chr's argument is ordinal, not
//                       narrower to integer-type alone, confirmed by reading
//                       its own pre-migration arm (issue #306's second
//                       slice), which used isOrdinal() for all three
//                       identically.
//   AK_StringLike       isVarStringLike() || isCharStringType() ||
//                       isShortStringLike() || Char || String -- the UNION
//                       of every string-like shape any dialect has, needed
//                       only by length (EP §6.7.6.7 / Turbo's own
//                       ShortString-length overload -- Builtins.def's own
//                       comment on Length) and EP's index, which shared one
//                       hand-written loop applying this exact union to
//                       EVERY argument before migration (index(s, t) checks
//                       both s and t identically, not just s).  isVarStringLike
//                       and isShortStringLike are individually dialect-exclusive
//                       (VarString cannot exist under -std=turbo, ShortString
//                       cannot exist under EP), so this union is equivalent
//                       to whichever narrower kind below applies under the
//                       active dialect -- it is spelled as a full union
//                       rather than picking one only because length/index's
//                       own pre-migration arm checked it as one, undifferentiated
//                       by dialect.
//   AK_EPStringLike     isVarStringLike() || isCharStringType() || Char ||
//                       String -- EP §6.7.6.7's canonical-string-type
//                       requirement, without ShortString: the narrower
//                       predicate trim and the six comparison functions
//                       (eq/ne/lt/gt/le/ge) already used by hand, deliberately
//                       NOT the AK_StringLike union above since neither is
//                       ever reachable under -std=turbo (checkEPOnly refuses
//                       the call before either arm runs), so widening to
//                       recognize a ShortString here would be dead code, not
//                       a real acceptance.
//   AK_TurboStringLike  isShortStringLike() || Char || String -- Turbo's own
//                       narrower string-like predicate (named isTurboStringLike
//                       at each of its pre-migration hand-written call sites,
//                       SemaExpr.cpp/SemaStmt.cpp), deliberately NOT
//                       isCharStringType: Turbo's own operators (checkBinary's
//                       Plus/comparison ShortString arms) never widen for the
//                       ISO/EP packed-array-of-char shape either, so neither
//                       does this.  Used where pos/concat's own pre-migration
//                       arms applied it uniformly to every argument (pos's two,
//                       concat's variadic list) -- copy and stringofchar also
//                       call isTurboStringLike, but only on ONE of their several
//                       differently-kinded arguments, which is exactly the
//                       "does not apply uniformly" shape that keeps a builtin
//                       hand-written (see below).
//   AK_File             T->Kind == TypeKind::File -- the plain file-typed-
//                       argument predicate eof/eoln (issue #261) and
//                       position/lastposition/empty (issue #417) already
//                       enforced by hand (err_file_argument) before this
//                       column existed.  Used only where EVERY argument in
//                       the call is File-shaped: eof/eoln (0 or 1 argument),
//                       position/lastposition/empty/filepos/filesize/
//                       seekeof/seekeoln/append/truncate/erase/flush (always
//                       exactly 1), and get/put/close (ISO §6.6.5.2/6.6.5.3's
//                       one-argument shape -- MaxArgs is -1 for these three,
//                       an old, unrelated arity looseness this column does
//                       not correct, but every argument any of them is ever
//                       actually called with is still File-typed).  NOT used
//                       for reset/rewrite/assign/rename/seek/blockread/
//                       blockwrite/settextbuf/extend/update/seekread/
//                       seekwrite/seekupdate, each of which mixes a File
//                       argument with a DIFFERENT kind (a filename, an index
//                       value, an untyped buffer, a transfer count, ...) in
//                       the same call -- issue #557 tracks the resulting gap
//                       (several of these never actually checked their own
//                       file argument's kind by hand either) as a separate,
//                       smaller bug fix rather than folding it into this
//                       refactor-only column.
//
// The numeric family (including EP's complex functions), ord/chr/odd of the
// ordinal family (issue #306's second slice), length/index/trim/eq/ne/lt/gt/
// le/ge/pos/concat of the string family (issue #306's third slice), and now
// eof/eoln/position/lastposition/empty/filepos/filesize/seekeof/seekeoln/
// append/truncate/erase/flush/get/put/close of the file family (issue #306's
// fourth slice) have been migrated to ArgKind -- the rest of AK_Any's
// special-cased builtins are exactly as before.  succ/pred are ALSO
// ordinal-argument-shaped in their first argument, but are deliberately NOT
// migrated: their optional second (step-count) argument requires a
// different kind (isIntegral(), EP §6.7.6.5) than their first, and
// checkBuiltinArgKinds applies one AK_* tag to every argument uniformly, so
// a builtin whose arguments do not all share one required kind has to stay
// hand-written (see the ArgKind column description above) -- this was
// anticipated in this same comment before any of the ordinal family was
// migrated.
//
// The third slice's own non-migrations, for the identical reason: substr
// (EP, string + two non-string index/count arguments), copy (Turbo, string +
// two non-string index/count arguments), stringofchar (Turbo, a Char argument
// that must be EXACTLY Char, not string-like, + a non-string count), and
// delete/insert/setlength/str/val (Turbo, each mixing a string argument with
// one or more var-parameter and/or non-string arguments, and Procs besides,
// checked in checkCallStmt rather than checkCallExpr) all stay hand-written.
// readstr/writestr (EP) were checked too and found to have no string-kind
// argument check to migrate at all -- their existing hand-written arm only
// enforces that specific argument POSITIONS are lvalues, never that any
// argument is string-like, so there is nothing here for ArgKind to replace.
// upcase (Turbo) takes a Char, not a string, and paramstr (Turbo) takes an
// Integer index -- neither is a member of the string family despite the
// "string" in their neighborhood, confirmed by reading each's own
// pre-migration arm before excluding it, the same diligence succ/pred's
// exclusion above already modeled.
//
// The fourth (file-family) slice's own non-migrations: reset/rewrite/assign/
// rename/seek/blockread/blockwrite/settextbuf/extend/update/seekread/
// seekwrite/seekupdate all mix a File argument with a different kind in the
// same call (see AK_File's own comment above) -- the identical "does not
// apply uniformly" shape substr/copy stayed hand-written for.  bind/unbind/
// binding stay hand-written for a DIFFERENT reason -- their real requirement
// is about the argument's underlying SYMBOL (an IdentExpr naming a bindable
// Var/VarParam), not just its resolved type, which checkBuiltinArgKinds
// cannot express at all (see their own row comment, above) -- the same
// context-sensitive shape that keeps SizeOf/High/Low/Assert/Exit/Break/
// Continue hand-written.  ioresult takes no arguments at all, so it is not a
// member of this family despite its neighborhood.  With this slice, every
// builtin family the issue's own scope named (numeric, ordinal, string,
// file) has been investigated; what remains AK_Any is either genuinely
// multi-kind, genuinely context-sensitive, or (ioresult, and every other
// builtin outside these four families) simply never had a migratable
// argument-kind check to begin with.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

#ifndef BUILTIN
#define BUILTIN(Id, Spelling, Kind, Dialects, MinArgs, MaxArgs, Result, ArgKind)
#endif

// The required procedures of ISO 7185 §6.6.5.  Their arguments are variadic
// (write), or are checked where they are lowered, so they carry no arity here.
#define ISO (LangOptions::D_ISO7185 | LangOptions::D_ISO10206)
#define EP  (LangOptions::D_ISO10206)
#define TP  (LangOptions::D_Turbo)
// Most of the required procedures and functions of ISO 7185 are Turbo's too;
// ALL is the ones every dialect has.  The five that are not -- page, get, put,
// pack and unpack -- belong to the ISO file model, which Turbo replaces with
// Assign and Seek rather than extends.  Naming them ISO here is what gets a
// Turbo program using `get` told what it is rather than that it is undefined.
#define ALL (ISO | TP)

BUILTIN(Write,     "write",     Proc, ALL, 0, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Writeln,   "writeln",   Proc, ALL, 0, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Read,      "read",      Proc, ALL, 0, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Readln,    "readln",    Proc, ALL, 0, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(New,       "new",       Proc, ALL, 1, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Dispose,   "dispose",   Proc, ALL, 1, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Page,      "page",      Proc, ISO, 0, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
// reset/rewrite take the file and, at most, an external filename (EP
// §6.7.5.2 / Turbo's record-size overload) -- CodeGen's lowering (CGProcCall,
// the reset/rewrite arm) only ever reads args[0] and args[1], so -1 here
// let a third argument through unexamined into a runtime call already typed
// for two.  Deliberately still AK_Any, not AK_File: the second argument is a
// DIFFERENT kind (a filename under EP, a record size under Turbo) from the
// first, and checkBuiltinArgKinds applies one tag to every argument in the
// call uniformly -- issue #306's fourth slice found this is the same
// position-specific shape that kept succ/pred (a different kind per
// argument) and substr/copy (a string mixed with non-string index/count
// arguments) hand-written in earlier slices.  reset/rewrite's own arg0 is
// NOT actually file-checked by hand either today (confirmed by reading
// checkCallStmt's own reset/rewrite arms before excluding this row, the same
// diligence the third slice's own substr/copy exclusion modeled) -- a
// pre-existing gap, not something this migration removes; tracked
// separately (issue #557) rather than hand-adding a brand new check under
// cover of a refactor-only slice.
BUILTIN(Reset,     "reset",     Proc, ALL, 1,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Rewrite,   "rewrite",   Proc, ALL, 1,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
// get/put/close: ISO §6.6.5.2/6.6.5.3's one-file-argument procedures, unlike
// reset/rewrite just above -- every argument these are ever legitimately
// called with is File-typed (confirmed empirically: no multi-argument
// get/put/close call exists anywhere in this project's own test suite), so
// AK_File applies uniformly even though MaxArgs is -1 rather than 1 (an old,
// unaudited looseness this slice does not tighten -- that is Builtins.def's
// ARITY column, a separate concern from ArgKind).  None of the three had ANY
// argument-kind check before this (confirmed by reading checkCallStmt/
// checkCallExpr, which special-case neither name at all) -- the SAME
// FileVarHelpers::fileVarPtr IdentExpr fast path issue #417 closed for
// position/lastposition/empty and #261 closed for eof/eoln hands back ANY
// variable's storage address unchecked for these three too, so `close(i)`
// for a plain integer i corrupts memory with no diagnostic.  Unlike
// reset/rewrite above, closing this gap needs no bespoke hand-written code
// at all: checkCallStmt's own generic fallback (issue #547) already calls
// checkBuiltinArgKinds for any builtin with no dedicated arm, so tagging
// these three AK_File is the whole fix.
BUILTIN(Get,       "get",       Proc, ISO, 1, -1, R_None, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Put,       "put",       Proc, ISO, 1, -1, R_None, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Close,     "close",     Proc, ALL, 1, -1, R_None, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Pack,      "pack",      Proc, ISO, 3,  3, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Unpack,    "unpack",    Proc, ISO, 3,  3, R_None, AK_Any)

// The required functions of ISO 7185 §6.6.6.
BUILTIN(Sqrt,      "sqrt",      Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Sin,       "sin",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Cos,       "cos",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Exp,       "exp",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Ln,        "ln",        Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Arctan,    "arctan",    Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Trunc,     "trunc",     Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Int, AK_NumericNonComplex)
BUILTIN(Round,     "round",     Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Int, AK_NumericNonComplex)
BUILTIN(Ord,       "ord",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Int, AK_Ordinal)
// abs and sqr take the type of their argument; R_Int is the placeholder the
// symbol carries, and the real result is worked out in checkCallExpr.
BUILTIN(Abs,       "abs",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Int, AK_Numeric)
BUILTIN(Sqr,       "sqr",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Int, AK_Numeric)
// EP §6.7.6.5 adds the second argument; ISO 7185 takes only the first, and the
// extra one is refused in checkCallExpr rather than by the arity here.  The
// first argument is ordinal-shaped exactly like ord/chr/odd below, but the
// second (optional) step count is a DIFFERENT required kind (isIntegral(),
// not isOrdinal()) -- checkBuiltinArgKinds has no per-position vocabulary, so
// this stays AK_Any and hand-written (see this file's header comment).
BUILTIN(Succ,      "succ",      Func, ALL, 1,  2, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Pred,      "pred",      Func, ALL, 1,  2, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Chr,       "chr",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Char, AK_Ordinal)
BUILTIN(Odd,       "odd",       Func, ALL, 1,  1, R_Bool, AK_Ordinal)
BUILTIN(Eof,       "eof",       Func, ALL, 0,  1, R_Bool, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Eoln,      "eoln",      Func, ALL, 0,  1, R_Bool, AK_File)

// ISO 10206 §6.7.5.7
BUILTIN(Halt,      "halt",      Proc, EP | TP, 0, 1, R_None, AK_Integer)
// EP §6.7.6.3
BUILTIN(Card,      "card",      Func, EP,  1,  1, R_Int, AK_Any)
// EP §6.7.6.7: strings
// Length is also Turbo's: real TP7/FPC's own System.Length(s) is a
// ShortString's length byte read back as an Integer -- discovered as a
// blocking gap while testing the System-unit string routines below (Copy/
// Delete/Insert/SetLength are meaningless to test without a way to ask a
// ShortString its own length), not a pre-existing, deliberate Turbo
// omission: no prior Turbo work landed a "length" of any kind, and nothing
// under -std=turbo could ask a ShortString's length at all before this.
BUILTIN(Length,    "length",    Func, EP | TP,  1,  1, R_Int, AK_StringLike)
BUILTIN(Index,     "index",     Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Int, AK_StringLike)
BUILTIN(Substr,    "substr",    Func, EP,  2,  3, R_Str, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Trim,      "trim",      Func, EP,  1,  1, R_Str, AK_EPStringLike)
// EP §6.7.6.7: string comparison, which differs from the relational operators
BUILTIN(StrEQ,     "eq",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
BUILTIN(StrNE,     "ne",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
BUILTIN(StrLT,     "lt",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
BUILTIN(StrGT,     "gt",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
BUILTIN(StrLE,     "le",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
BUILTIN(StrGE,     "ge",        Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Bool, AK_EPStringLike)
// EP §6.7.5.5
BUILTIN(Readstr,   "readstr",   Proc, EP,  2, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Writestr,  "writestr",  Proc, EP,  2, -1, R_None, AK_Any)
// EP §6.7.6.3 / §6.7.6.2: complex
BUILTIN(Cmplx,     "cmplx",     Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Complex, AK_NumericNonComplex)
BUILTIN(Polar,     "polar",     Func, EP,  2,  2, R_Complex, AK_NumericNonComplex)
BUILTIN(Re,        "re",        Func, EP,  1,  1, R_Real, AK_Complex)
BUILTIN(Im,        "im",        Func, EP,  1,  1, R_Real, AK_Complex)
BUILTIN(Arg,       "arg",       Func, EP,  1,  1, R_Real, AK_Complex)
// EP §6.7.5.2: direct-access files.  Same two-argument shape as reset/rewrite
// above, and the same reason it is 2 rather than -1.  Extend/Update stay
// AK_Any for the identical multi-kind reason reset/rewrite do (arg1 is a
// filename, a different kind from arg0's File); SeekRead/SeekWrite/
// SeekUpdate stay AK_Any because THEIR arg1 is a value of the file's own
// declared INDEX type (EP §6.7.5.2), not File either -- also multi-kind, and
// also not File-checked on arg0 by hand today (no dedicated Sema arm exists
// for any of these five names at all) -- tracked with reset/rewrite's own
// identical gap, issue #557, rather than hand-added here.
BUILTIN(Extend,    "extend",    Proc, EP,  1,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Update,    "update",    Proc, EP,  1,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(SeekRead,  "seekread",  Proc, EP,  2,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(SeekWrite, "seekwrite", Proc, EP,  2,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(SeekUpdate,"seekupdate",Proc, EP,  2,  2, R_None, AK_Any)
// EP §6.7.6.6 / §6.7.6.5: each takes a file argument alone and had no
// argument-kind check of its own before issue #417 added one by hand
// (err_file_argument) -- generic now (issue #306's fourth slice).
BUILTIN(Position,     "position",     Func, EP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_File)
BUILTIN(LastPosition, "lastposition", Func, EP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Empty,        "empty",        Func, EP, 1, 1, R_Bool, AK_File)
// EP §6.7.5.8 / §6.7.6.9: date and time
BUILTIN(GetTimeStamp, "gettimestamp", Proc, EP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Date,         "date",         Func, EP, 1, 1, R_Str, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Time,         "time",         Func, EP, 1, 1, R_Str, AK_Any)
// EP §6.7.5.6 / §6.7.6.8: binding.  All three stay AK_Any and hand-written
// (Sema::checkBindingCall, Sema.cpp) despite each taking what looks like a
// plain file argument: the real requirement is narrower and context-
// sensitive in the same way SizeOf/High/Low's type-or-value shape is -- the
// first argument must be an IdentExpr naming a SymbolKind::Var/VarParam
// SYMBOL directly (not an arbitrary File-typed expression -- a record field
// or array element of file type is refused, unlike every AK_File builtin
// above), that symbol's own IsBindable flag must be set, AND its declared
// type must be File -- three checks with three different diagnostics
// (err_bind_needs_variable / err_bind_not_bindable /
// err_bind_nonfile_unsupported), none of which checkBuiltinArgKinds's
// generic "call checkExpr, test the resolved TYPE" shape can express (it has
// no access to the underlying Symbol at all, only the expression's type).
BUILTIN(Bind,         "bind",         Proc, EP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Unbind,       "unbind",       Proc, EP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Binding,      "binding",      Func, EP, 1, 1, R_BindingType, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: this file's first genuinely TP-only entry -- everything
// above either serves every dialect (ALL) or, like Halt, is EP|TP.  Real
// Borland/FPC's Assert(cond: Boolean [; msg: string]) -- gated at the call
// site on the Assertions switch (CompilerSwitches.def), which CGProcCall's
// emitCallStmt reads through Opts.switchOn(Switch::Assertions, s.Loc) rather
// than anything recorded here: {$C-} makes the whole call compile to
// nothing, not even evaluating cond (confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`: a
// condition with an observable side effect never runs it once assertions are
// off), which the arity/dialect gate every OTHER builtin gets from this table
// alone cannot express.
BUILTIN(Assert,       "assert",       Proc, TP, 1, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// TP RunError([errorcode: Integer]) -- aborts the program the same way one
// of the numbered runtime checks (RangeCheckGuards.cpp/plang_tp_runerror)
// would, printing "Runtime error <n> at $<addr>" and exiting with status n
// itself.  The no-argument form defaults to error code 0 -- confirmed
// against `fpc -Mtp`: RunError with nothing already pending reports
// "Runtime error 0" and exits 0, not 216 or any other check's own number.
// No special Sema arm (unlike Assert, just above): RunError needs no
// {$C-}-style gate on whether or how its argument is evaluated, so the
// generic "arity from this table, evaluate arguments, dispatch spelling in
// codegen" path every other builtin without a special shape already takes
// (Halt(n) is the closest precedent: same 0-or-1 Integer argument shape)
// is all this needs.
BUILTIN(RunError,     "runerror",     Proc, TP, 0, 1, R_None, AK_Integer)

// -std=turbo only: Exit/Break/Continue, TP's three statement-level
// control-transfer builtins -- no ISO 7185/EP §6.6.5 or §6.7.5 equivalent.
// Reached through CallStmt exactly like Halt/Assert above (CGProcCall::
// emitCallStmt dispatches all three on spelling, the same mechanism), never
// through CallExpr: Exit's optional value is FPC's accepted extension to
// strict Delphi's argument-less form (Kind Proc despite taking a value --
// it is a statement, not an expression, and 0/1 here is only the ARITY
// Builtins.def can express; whether a given call's argument is legal at all
// depends on whether the enclosing block is a function, which only
// checkCallStmt's own arm can decide, the same way Assert's {$C-} gating
// could not be expressed here either).  Break/Continue take no argument and
// are refused outside a loop; Exit's value is refused outside a function.
// Both restrictions are new diagnostics (err_break_outside_loop /
// err_continue_outside_loop / err_exit_value_outside_function,
// DiagnosticSemaKinds.def), not Builtins.def's arity/dialect gate alone.
BUILTIN(Exit,         "exit",         Proc, TP, 0, 1, R_None, AK_Any)
// Turbo Tier 5, Cluster A item 6: 'Fail' inside a constructor -- see
// Sema::checkCallStmt's own 'fail' arm (SemaStmt.cpp) and
// err_fail_outside_constructor for the context restriction Builtins.def
// alone cannot express (confirmed against a local fpc -Mtp build that
// 'Fail' is not even a recognized identifier outside a constructor).
BUILTIN(Fail,          "fail",         Proc, TP, 0, 0, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Break,        "break",        Proc, TP, 0, 0, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Continue,     "continue",     Proc, TP, 0, 0, R_None, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: Assigned(p) -- true iff p, a pointer or a procedural
// value, is not nil.  Real Borland/FPC's own Assigned; plang's checkCallExpr/
// checkCallStmt each have a dedicated arm restricting the argument to a
// pointer or procedural (Procedure/Function) type, matching the shape check
// every other single-typed-argument builtin above (Card, Odd, ...) gets.
BUILTIN(Assigned,     "assigned",     Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Bool, AK_Any)

// Turbo Tier 5, Cluster A item 7: TypeOf(anObjectValueOrVar) -- the address
// of the object's own VMT (Codegen::Impl::getOrCreateVmt's existing global),
// confirmed against a local fpc -Mtp build to be exactly what field practice
// compares with '=' to ask "is this object really a TDog" ('if TypeOf(D) =
// TypeOf(TDog2) then' for two separate TDog instances is true; 'TypeOf(A) =
// TypeOf(D)' for an ancestor instance and a descendant one is false).
// R_None is a placeholder exactly like SizeOf/High/Low's own just below --
// checkUserDefinedCall's dedicated arm returns TypeContext::
// getGenericPointer() directly, the same generic 'Pointer' type real
// TypeOf's own FPC declaration ('function TypeOf(...): Pointer') has.
BUILTIN(TypeOf,       "typeof",       Func, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: SizeOf/High/Low take a TYPE NAME as well as (like real
// FPC) an ordinary value expression -- the one argument SHAPE in this whole
// file that is not a plain expression; see Parser::parseSizeHighLowArg and
// Sema::resolveTypeArgOrValue (SemaExpr.cpp) for the two paths.  R_Int is a
// placeholder the same way Abs/Sqr's is just above: SizeOf is always an
// integer, but High/Low answer in the ARGUMENT's own type (High(Byte) is a
// Byte), which only checkCallExpr's dedicated arm can work out.
BUILTIN(SizeOf,       "sizeof",       Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(High,         "high",         Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Low,          "low",          Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)
// -std=turbo only: FPC's size-aware Hi/Lo/Swap -- deliberately NOT literal
// Turbo Pascal 7's, which only ever worked on a 16-bit value; see
// CGFuncCall::emitBuiltinCall's own comment for the field-practice
// citation and Sema::checkCallExpr's Hi/Lo/Swap arm for the divergence
// itself.  R_Int is a placeholder for the same reason High/Low's is: Hi and
// Lo answer in half of the argument's own width, and Swap in the argument's
// own type, none of which this table alone can express.
BUILTIN(Hi,           "hi",           Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Lo,           "lo",           Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Swap,         "swap",         Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: Random()/Random(Range) -- the RTL's pseudo-random number
// generator, sharing one name across TWO call shapes the way Hi/Lo/Swap just
// above share their own R_Int placeholder: the zero-argument form answers a
// REAL in [0, 1); the one-argument form advances the SAME generator state
// (RandSeed, a predefined Var registered the same way ExitCode is --
// Sema::registerBuiltins) and answers an INTEGER-kind value in [0, Range)
// instead, in the ARGUMENT's own type -- the same "stays in the argument's
// own type" rule Abs/Sqr/Succ/Pred/High/Low already follow.  Neither shape
// is the single fixed Result this table alone can express; R_Real here is
// only the zero-argument answer, and Sema::checkCallExpr's own dedicated arm
// (mirroring Abs/Sqr's identical "argument decides" shape) overrides it for
// the one-argument form.  This is plang's OWN generator (runtime/
// plang_math.cpp's plang_tp_random_real/_range) -- no claim is made that its
// sequence matches real Borland Turbo Pascal 7's (its own 32-bit LCG) or
// Free Pascal's (a Mersenne Twister) -- the two do not match each other
// either, and neither is worth chasing bit-for-bit.
BUILTIN(Random,       "random",       Func, TP, 0,  1, R_Real, AK_Any)
// Randomize -- reseeds RandSeed from a real source of entropy (wall-clock
// time via clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)), so successive RUNS of the same
// program get a different Random sequence; runtime/plang_math.cpp's
// plang_tp_randomize.
BUILTIN(Randomize,    "randomize",    Proc, TP, 0,  0, R_None, AK_Any)
// Int(x)/Frac(x) -- x's integer part, toward zero like Trunc, but a REAL
// result rather than an ordinal one, so -- unlike Trunc/Round -- there is no
// int64 range to respect: Int(1e30) is simply 1e30, not the runtime error
// Trunc(1e30) correctly is.  Frac(x) = x - Int(x), x's fractional part with
// x's own sign (Frac(-3.7) is -0.7, not 0.3 -- there is no floor here).  See
// runtime/plang_math.cpp's plang_tp_int/plang_tp_frac for why these are NOT
// built on top of the existing, range-checked plang_trunc/plang_round.
BUILTIN(Int,          "int",          Func, TP, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_NumericNonComplex)
BUILTIN(Frac,         "frac",         Func, TP, 1,  1, R_Real, AK_NumericNonComplex)

// -std=turbo only: Include(s, x) / Exclude(s, x) -- `s := s + [x]` and
// `s := s - [x]` by another name, mutating the set variable in place.
BUILTIN(Include,      "include",      Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Exclude,      "exclude",      Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// -std=turbo only: Inc(x[, n]) / Dec(x[, n]) -- mutate an ordinal variable
// (or, for a PChar-like typed pointer, advance/retreat it the same way
// `p + n`/`p - n` already do) in place by n, defaulting to 1.
BUILTIN(Inc,          "inc",          Proc, TP, 1, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Dec,          "dec",          Proc, TP, 1, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// -std=turbo only: FillChar(var X; Count: Integer; Value) and
// Move(const Source; var Dest; Count: Integer) -- X/Source/Dest are
// "untyped" the way real Turbo Pascal's are: any variable, addressed
// directly, its own declared type not otherwise examined.  Count is a BYTE
// count in both, matching FPC field practice.
BUILTIN(FillChar,     "fillchar",     Proc, TP, 3, 3, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Move,         "move",         Proc, TP, 3, 3, R_None, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: the System-unit ShortString routines and Str/Val.
// Deliberately NOT LowerCase/UpperCase (confirmed absent from real TP7's
// System unit -- SysUtils-era additions, out of this milestone) and NOT
// Trim/Index/Substr (those already exist above as EP-only builtins with
// EP-specific semantics -- an empty-pattern `index` returns 1, EP's substr
// RAISES out of range -- that must not be disturbed or reused here).
//
// Copy/Concat/StringOfChar all return a capacity-255 ShortString, R_Str's
// placeholder overridden the same way Sqr/Abs/High/Low's R_Int placeholder
// is: their real signatures in FPC's own System unit are `function Copy(...):
// string` etc, i.e. the DEFAULT capacity, regardless of any input operand's
// own declared (possibly narrower) string[N] capacity -- confirmed against
// `fpc -Mtp` (Copy/Concat of two string[5] operands is still a full
// string[255] until truncated by a later assignment, not string[10]).
BUILTIN(Copy,         "copy",         Func, TP, 3, 3, R_Str, AK_Any)
// Pos(substr, s) -- 1-based index of the first occurrence of substr in s, or
// 0 if not found.  EP's `index` returns 1 for an EMPTY pattern (ISO 10206's
// own rule); Pos does NOT share that rule -- confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`:
// Pos('', s) is 0 for every s, empty or not.  A completely separate runtime
// entry point (plang_sstr_pos) from EP's plang_str_index for exactly this
// reason, even though the search algorithm is otherwise the same shape.
BUILTIN(Pos,          "pos",          Func, TP, 2, 2, R_Int, AK_TurboStringLike)
// Concat(s1 [, s2, ..., sn]) -- variadic concatenation, same capacity-255
// result and same clamping the `+` operator's own ShortString arm already
// implements (CGBinaryOps.cpp); MaxArgs -1 since real Turbo/FPC's Concat
// takes one or more strings, not exactly two.
BUILTIN(Concat,       "concat",       Func, TP, 1, -1, R_Str, AK_TurboStringLike)
// Delete(var s: string; index, count: Integer) -- removes count characters
// from s starting at index, MUTATING s in place.  Unlike Copy's index/count
// clamping, an out-of-range index (< 1 or > Length(s)) is a silent NO-OP,
// not clamped to the nearest valid position -- confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`
// (Delete(s, 0, 2) and Delete(s, 100, 3) both leave s completely unchanged).
BUILTIN(Delete,       "delete",       Proc, TP, 3, 3, R_None, AK_Any)
// Insert(source: string; var s: string; index: Integer) -- inserts source
// into s before position index, MUTATING s in place, clamped at s's own
// declared capacity.  Unlike Delete, an out-of-range index IS clamped (to 1
// or to Length(s)+1) rather than making the call a no-op -- confirmed
// against `fpc -Mtp`; see CGProcCall.cpp's own comment for the full
// empirically-derived clamp table.
BUILTIN(Insert,       "insert",       Proc, TP, 3, 3, R_None, AK_Any)
// SetLength(var s: string; newLength: Integer) -- sets s's own length byte
// directly, clamped to s's declared capacity (0 if newLength < 0).  Bytes
// exposed by growing are left whatever they already held -- confirmed
// against `fpc -Mtp` that real Turbo/FPC does not zero or space-pad them
// either.  Deliberately DOES NOT reproduce one further fpc quirk also
// confirmed empirically: real `fpc -Mtp` lets SetLength write a length byte
// past a narrow string[N]'s own physical (N+1)-byte storage with no clamp at
// all (SetLength(s5, 100) on a string[5] left Length(s5) = 100 and a later
// s5[6] read/wrote out of bounds, `Runtime error 201` under `-Cr`) -- an
// actual buffer overrun in real Turbo/FPC's implementation, not a rule worth
// matching; plang clamps to the declared capacity here the same way every
// other plang_sstr_* function already does.
BUILTIN(SetLength,    "setlength",    Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// StringOfChar(ch: Char; count: Integer) -- a capacity-255 ShortString of
// count copies of ch, clamped at 255 (StringOfChar('x', 300) is 255 x's, not
// a runtime error) the same way every other ShortString-producing routine
// here clamps rather than errors.
BUILTIN(StringOfChar, "stringofchar", Func, TP, 2, 2, R_Str, AK_Any)
// UpCase(ch: Char): Char -- real Turbo Pascal 7's UpCase takes and returns a
// CHAR, not a string (that two-argument-shape confusion is a later Delphi/
// SysUtils-era overload this milestone excludes, matching the Lower/
// UpperCase exclusion above).  Only 'a'..'z' change; every other character,
// ASCII or not, passes through unchanged.
BUILTIN(UpCase,       "upcase",       Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Char, AK_Any)
// Str(x [: width [: decimals]]; var s: string) -- formats x the same way
// write(x [: width [: decimals]]) already does (reusing the writestr capture
// machinery, BuiltinIO.cpp), into ShortString destination s.  x's optional
// width/decimals are parsed the same way write's own are (ParseStmt.cpp adds
// "str" to the write-parameter spelling set for exactly this).
BUILTIN(Str,          "str",          Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// Val(s: string; var v; var code: Integer) -- parses s as v's own type
// (integer or real) into v, and sets code to 0 on success or to the 1-based
// index of the first character that does not fit Val's grammar on failure.
// NEVER aborts the process on bad input, unlike plang_read_i64/_f64's
// err_read_format/err_read_int_range ([[noreturn]], plang_io.cpp) -- this is
// Val's entire reason for being a separate, new, non-fatal parsing primitive
// (runtime/plang_val.cpp) rather than a thin reuse of readstr's existing
// abort-on-malformed-input machinery.
BUILTIN(Val,          "val",          Proc, TP, 3, 3, R_None, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: GetMem(var P: Pointer; Size: Int64) / FreeMem(P: Pointer
// [, Size: Int64]) -- runtime/plang_sys.cpp's plang_tp_getmem/
// plang_tp_freemem, a wholly separate, NON-ABORTING pair of allocation entry
// points from New/Dispose's plang_new/plang_dispose (that file's own top
// comment on why this is a genuinely separate pair, not a flag).  Size is
// Int64, not real Borland TP7's 16-bit Word -- matches `fpc -Mtp` field
// practice (its own rtl/inc/heap.inc widened GetMem's Size well past 65535
// even in TP-compat mode) and HeapError's own Size parameter
// (Sema::registerBuiltins), for the same reason a mismatched pair would be
// worse than either alone.
BUILTIN(GetMem,       "getmem",       Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(FreeMem,      "freemem",      Proc, TP, 1, 2, R_None, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: ParamCount/ParamStr(n) -- read back the argc/argv every
// compiled program's own C main now receives (CodeGenProcs.cpp's emitMain,
// runtime/plang_sys.cpp's plang_set_args).  ParamStr returns a capacity-255
// ShortString, the same result type Copy/Concat/StringOfChar above do.
BUILTIN(ParamCount,   "paramcount",   Func, TP, 0, 0, R_Int, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(ParamStr,     "paramstr",     Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Str, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: IOResult -- reads InOutRes (Sema::registerBuiltins'
// InOutRes Symbol, runtime/plang_sys.cpp's plang_tp_inoutres) and CLEARS IT
// TO ZERO in the same call, real Turbo Pascal's own read-and-clear contract
// -- see plang_tp_ioresult's own comment (runtime/plang_sys.cpp) for the
// confirmed field practice and why the read-and-clear itself happens only
// there, never duplicated in CodeGen.  Zero args, Func, TP-only: the exact
// same shape ParamCount just above already has, which is what gets a bare,
// parenthesis-less `x := IOResult;` the correct dialect gating "for free"
// through Sema::checkIdent's generic SymbolKind::Builtin case (its own
// checkEPOnly call, closed for exactly this shape of builtin by the fix
// ParamCount/Random's own arrival there required) -- confirmed for
// IOResult specifically, not just assumed to transfer, by this item's own
// dialect-gating lit test.  AK_Any is not a placeholder here awaiting
// migration -- IOResult takes NO arguments at all (MinArgs = MaxArgs = 0),
// so it is not a file-argument builtin despite living in this neighborhood;
// there is no argument for any ArgKind tag, File included, to ever check.
BUILTIN(IOResult,     "ioresult",     Func, TP, 0, 0, R_Int, AK_Any)

// -std=turbo only: Assign(var f; const name: string) / Append(var f: Text).
// TP's own file model, which replaces (not extends) ISO/EP's reset/rewrite/
// extend/update/bind: Reset/Rewrite/Append here take no filename argument
// of their own at all -- they open whatever Assign last bound f to (an
// empty name means "the console": stdin for Reset, stdout for Rewrite and
// Append).  See CGProcCall.cpp's own dispatch and runtime/plang_file.cpp's
// plang_tp_assign/plang_tp_reset/plang_tp_rewrite/plang_tp_append/
// plang_tp_close for the whole design -- a genuinely separate function
// family from the ISO ones just above, per this project's P7 rule that
// dialect selection happens at the CALL SITE, never inside one runtime
// function branching on a passed-in "which dialect" flag.  Assign stays
// AK_Any: its own arg0 File check is already hand-written (err_file_argument,
// checkCallStmt), but arg1 is a DIFFERENT kind (the filename, string-like),
// so the whole call cannot take one uniform AK_* tag -- the same
// reset/rewrite shape, issue #557.  Append, unlike Assign, takes ONLY the
// file (Builtins.def's own MaxArgs = 1) -- genuinely uniform, so it is
// migrated (AK_File), split out of the hand-written arm it used to share
// with Assign (SemaStmt.cpp) into its own.
BUILTIN(Assign,       "assign",       Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Append,       "append",       Proc, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_File)

// -std=turbo only: FilePos(f) / FileSize(f) -- current / total record
// position, in units of f's own RecSize (Cluster A item 4's RecSize
// wiring: SizeOf(element) for a typed file, the Reset/Rewrite-given or
// default-128 record size for an untyped one), NOT bytes.  R_Int is only
// the placeholder Builtins.def's own convention uses for a result whose
// real type is worked out elsewhere (the comment on Abs/Sqr, above, is the
// original example) -- checkCallExpr overrides it to a genuine 64-bit
// Int64 here, wide enough that a >2GB file's FileSize does not wrap the
// way Turbo's own 32-bit LongInt eventually would.  Both refuse a Text
// argument (err_binary_file_required) -- confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`,
// which refuses to call either with one at all.  The base File-kind check
// is generic now (AK_File, issue #306's fourth slice); the binary-only
// restriction is not a KIND (only ever one argument here to begin with), so
// it stays a hand-written follow-on check exactly like Eoln's own text-only
// restriction does.
BUILTIN(FilePos,      "filepos",      Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_File)
BUILTIN(FileSize,     "filesize",     Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Int, AK_File)
// -std=turbo only: SeekEof(f) / SeekEoln(f) -- the CONSUMING counterparts
// of Eof/Eoln: SeekEof skips past any blanks, tabs and line markers ahead
// of the current position before testing; SeekEoln skips only blanks and
// tabs (a line marker is itself what Eoln tests for, so SeekEoln does not
// cross one).  Text-only, the same restriction Eoln itself already has
// (err_line_proc_not_text) -- confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`.  Base File-kind
// check generic now (AK_File); text-only restriction hand-written, same
// reasoning as FilePos/FileSize's binary-only one just above.
BUILTIN(SeekEof,      "seekeof",      Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Bool, AK_File)
BUILTIN(SeekEoln,     "seekeoln",     Func, TP, 1, 1, R_Bool, AK_File)

// -std=turbo only: Seek(f, n) / Truncate(f) -- position f at record n
// (0-relative, in units of RecSize) / truncate f at the current position.
// Both refuse a Text argument (err_binary_file_required), the same as
// FilePos/FileSize above.  Seek stays AK_Any: arg1 (n) is a DIFFERENT kind
// (Integer) from arg0's File, the same multi-kind shape as reset/rewrite,
// so its whole hand-written arm (both arguments) is unchanged.  Truncate,
// unlike Seek, takes ONLY the file (MaxArgs = 1) -- genuinely uniform, so it
// is migrated (AK_File), split out of the arm it used to share with Seek
// (SemaStmt.cpp) into its own, keeping the binary-only restriction
// hand-written the same way FilePos/FileSize/SeekEof/SeekEoln's own do.
BUILTIN(Seek,         "seek",         Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(Truncate,     "truncate",     Proc, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_File)
// -std=turbo only: BlockRead(f, var buf; count[; var result]) /
// BlockWrite(f, var buf; count[; var result]) -- transfer count
// RecSize-sized records between f and buf, an UNTYPED var parameter (any
// variable at all, addressed directly, the same as FillChar/Move's own X/
// Source/Dest above).  The optional 4th argument is not cosmetic: WITH a
// result argument, a short transfer is not an error -- result simply
// receives the actual count transferred; WITHOUT one, a short transfer
// sets InOutRes to 100 (BlockRead)/101 (BlockWrite) -- Borland's own
// documented distinction, confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`.  See
// CGProcCall.cpp's own dispatch for exactly where that arity reads.  Stays
// AK_Any: three (or four) genuinely different kinds in one call -- File,
// then an untyped lvalue, then an Integer count, then an optional Integer
// result -- the same multi-kind shape that keeps BlockRead/BlockWrite's
// whole hand-written arm as it was.
BUILTIN(BlockRead,    "blockread",    Proc, TP, 3, 4, R_None, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(BlockWrite,   "blockwrite",   Proc, TP, 3, 4, R_None, AK_Any)
// -std=turbo only: Erase(f) / Rename(f, newname) -- delete / rename the
// file f is bound to (by Assign), and (Rename only) update f's own bound
// name to match.  Both require f to be fmClosed first -- confirmed against
// `fpc -Mtp`: either call against a still-open f sets InOutRes to 102
// ("file not assigned" -- FPC's own field practice reuses that code here
// rather than a dedicated one) and performs nothing.  Erase takes only the
// file -- genuinely uniform, migrated (AK_File).  Rename stays AK_Any: its
// second argument (newname) is string-like, a different kind from File, the
// same multi-kind shape as Assign's own filename argument just above.
BUILTIN(Erase,        "erase",        Proc, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_File)
BUILTIN(Rename,       "rename",       Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
// -std=turbo only: Flush(f) -- flush f's buffered output without closing
// it.  Issue #739: an earlier version of this comment claimed no
// Text/binary restriction ("confirmed against `fpc -Mtp`: both accept
// it") -- re-checked against a local fpc -Mtp 3.2.2 install and that does
// NOT hold; fpc accepts only Text, refusing both typed and untyped files
// ("Call by var for arg no. 1 has to match exactly: ... expected 'Text'").
// One argument, one kind (AK_File) for the base File/not-a-File check;
// SemaStmt.cpp's own "flush" arm adds the Text-only restriction on top,
// the same split Truncate's binary-only restriction has.
BUILTIN(Flush,        "flush",        Proc, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_File)
// -std=turbo only: SetTextBuf(var f: Text; var buf[; size]) -- overrides
// f's own internal I/O buffer with caller-supplied storage.  buf is an
// UNTYPED var parameter like BlockRead/BlockWrite's own above; size
// defaults to 1024 when omitted (real Borland's own default is
// SizeOf(buf), which plang cannot know for an untyped actual with no
// further help -- see CGProcCall.cpp's own comment for why a fixed default
// is used instead).  Text-only (err_line_proc_not_text), the same
// restriction Eoln/SeekEoln already have.  Stays AK_Any: File, then an
// untyped lvalue, then an optional Integer -- the same multi-kind shape as
// BlockRead/BlockWrite just above.
BUILTIN(SetTextBuf,   "settextbuf",   Proc, TP, 2, 3, R_None, AK_Any)

// Turbo Tier 4, Cluster C item 5: real TP's own Crt unit's three routines
// that genuinely need OS support (a real wait; raw, unbuffered keyboard
// input) rather than being pure Pascal built on top of Write, the way
// ClrScr/GotoXY/TextColor/... are (see share/plang/units/Crt.pas).  These
// are declared as ordinary dialect-wide TP builtins -- reachable with no
// `uses Crt` -- rather than as identifiers scoped to the Crt unit's own
// interface, because this compiler has no mechanism yet for a Pascal-level
// unit interface declaration to bind to a NEW runtime entry point (every
// existing one, this list included, is dispatched by CodeGen matching the
// call's spelling, in a single global table Sema::registerBuiltins fills
// once per compile -- there is no per-unit equivalent).  Real Turbo Pascal
// itself already blurs this same line for Random/Randomize/ParamStr/ParamCount/
// Halt above, which are actually System unit routines needing no `uses` at
// all despite "belonging" to a specific unit conceptually; Delay/KeyPressed/
// ReadKey follow that exact, already-established precedent rather than
// inventing a new one. A documented, deliberate simplification -- not an
// oversight -- see this item's own report for the full reasoning.
BUILTIN(Delay,        "delay",        Proc, TP, 1, 1, R_None, AK_Integer)
BUILTIN(KeyPressed,   "keypressed",   Func, TP, 0, 0, R_Bool, AK_Any)
BUILTIN(ReadKey,      "readkey",      Func, TP, 0, 0, R_Char, AK_Any)

// Issue #704: WhereX/WhereY only tracked GotoXY/ClrScr, never ordinary
// Write/Writeln output.  The fix keeps a software cursor model in the
// RUNTIME (runtime/plang_io.cpp's own plangOutN, the one place all
// unqualified Write/Writeln output funnels through), since only the
// runtime ever sees the actual bytes written -- Crt.pas itself has no way
// to intercept a plain `Write` call.  These three are NOT real Turbo
// Pascal identifiers (unlike Delay/KeyPressed/ReadKey just above, which
// mirror real TP's own dialect-wide System-unit routines): they exist
// purely so share/plang/units/Crt.pas's own GotoXY/ClrScr/WhereX/WhereY
// can reach that runtime state at all, for the exact "no mechanism for a
// unit interface declaration to bind to a NEW runtime entry point" reason
// documented on Delay/KeyPressed/ReadKey above -- deliberately obscure
// spellings so a real program's own identifiers are in no realistic danger
// of colliding.  CrtSyncCursor(X, Y) is called from GotoXY/ClrScr with the
// exact window-relative coordinates Crt.pas already computes, whenever
// those explicitly move the cursor; CrtTrackedX/CrtTrackedY read the
// tracked column/row back (Int64, like ParamCount/IOResult above; Crt.pas
// truncates into its own Byte-returning WhereX/WhereY the same way it
// already truncated its own Integer-typed CursorX/CursorY into them).
BUILTIN(CrtSyncCursor, "crtsynccursor", Proc, TP, 2, 2, R_None, AK_Any)
